course: myths
lesson: 20
title: Explorers, scientists and inventors
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Mrs Brown tells the class that in Columbus's time everyone thought the world was flat, and your older cousin grins that Einstein failed maths. Both stories are told again and again, and both are wrong.
explain: We love a story with one lone hero and one magic moment: an apple bonks Newton on the head, Edison alone lights up the world, a kite gets hit by lightning. Real discovery is slower and shared.
explain: Most big ideas were built by many people over many years: someone made the first try, others improved it, and one name got famous because it made a good story or sold well.
explain: Educated Europeans knew the Earth was round two thousand years before Columbus; the argument was about how BIG it was, and Columbus got that badly wrong.
explain: To check a hero story, ask "who did this before?" and "who helped?"; the answer usually makes the hero more human, not less great.
fact: Educated people in Europe knew the Earth was round since ancient Greek times; Eratosthenes estimated its size more than 2,200 years ago.
fact: Columbus's critics argued that the Earth was much bigger than he claimed, and they were right; he was sailing west to reach Asia, which was far too far for his ships' food and water, and he only survived because the Americas lay in the way.
fact: The idea that people in Columbus's time thought the Earth was flat was spread in the 1800s, partly by a popular book by Washington Irving.
fact: Einstein did not fail maths; he was very good at it as a boy, and the myth may come from a failed entrance exam he took two years early, where he did well in maths and physics but not in other subjects.
fact: Newton said seeing an apple fall from a tree helped him think about gravity; there is no evidence it hit him on the head.
fact: Many inventors made electric light bulbs before Edison, including Joseph Swan in England; Edison's team at Menlo Park made a long-lasting, practical bulb and a whole system to power it.
fact: Indigenous peoples (the first peoples of a land), millions of them, had lived in the Americas for many thousands of years before Columbus, and Norse sailors led by Leif Erikson reached North America around the year 1000.
fact: Columbus first landed in the Bahamas in 1492 and never reached the land that is now the United States.
fact: The telescope was invented in the Netherlands in 1608; Galileo built better ones and in 1609 and 1610 turned them to the sky, discovering Jupiter's moons.
fact: Henri Becquerel discovered radioactivity in 1896; Marie Curie named it, studied it, and with Pierre Curie discovered polonium and radium; she won two Nobel Prizes.
fact: In Benjamin Franklin's kite experiment, reported in 1752, the kite collected electric charge from storm clouds; he was not struck by lightning, which would very likely have killed him, and some historians doubt the experiment happened as told.
fact: Walt Disney was not frozen; he was cremated (his body was burned to ashes, as many families choose) after he died in 1966.
fact: The Wright brothers made the first powered, controlled, sustained (kept going, not just a hop) flight in an aeroplane in 1903; people had already flown in hot-air balloons since 1783 and in gliders.
exercise: What did educated Europeans think about the shape of the Earth in Columbus's time? | That it was round, as the ancient Greeks had shown | That it was flat, with a sharp edge that ships could fall off | That it was shaped like a long sausage | That nobody could ever know its shape
exercise: What were Columbus's critics really arguing about? | The size of the Earth, which he made far too small | Whether the Earth was flat or round, which nobody yet knew | Whether ships could float on salty water | Whether the Sun went round the Earth
exercise: Who was right about the size of the Earth? | Columbus's critics, who said it was much bigger | Columbus, who said it was quite small | Nobody, because they all thought it was flat | The king, who said it was exactly as big as Spain
exercise: When did the "everyone thought the world was flat" story become popular? | In the 1800s, helped by a popular book | In ancient Greece, in the time of Eratosthenes | In 1492, in a letter from Columbus himself | In the 1960s, when photos from space appeared
exercise: Did Einstein fail maths at school? | No, he was very good at maths as a boy | Yes, he failed every maths test he ever took | Yes, but only when he was at university | No, because his school did not teach maths
exercise: What did Newton say about the apple? | Seeing one fall helped him think about gravity | One fell right on his head and knocked him out cold | He threw one high up in the air to test his idea of gravity | He never mentioned an apple at all, in any of his writing
exercise: Who made electric light bulbs before Edison? | Many inventors, including Joseph Swan in England | Nobody; Edison made the very first one all by himself | Only Benjamin Franklin with his kite | Galileo, who used them in his telescopes
exercise: What did Edison's team really achieve? | A long-lasting, practical bulb and a system to power it | The very first idea of electric light in the whole of history | The discovery of electricity in lightning | A bulb that never needed any electricity
exercise: Who lived in the Americas before Columbus arrived? | Indigenous peoples, for many thousands of years | Nobody; the land was completely empty | Only Spanish farmers who had sailed over many years before | Only Norse sailors, from the year 1000
exercise: Where did Columbus first land in 1492? | The Bahamas | New York | Canada | California
exercise: What did Galileo really do with the telescope? | He improved it and turned it to the sky | He invented it from nothing in 1608 | He used it only to look at ships at sea | He banned it because it showed too much
exercise: Who discovered radioactivity in 1896? | Henri Becquerel | Marie Curie | Albert Einstein | Thomas Edison
exercise: What happened to Benjamin Franklin's kite in the story? | It collected electric charge from storm clouds | It was hit by a huge bolt of lightning and burst into flames | It flew so high on the wind that it nearly reached outer space | It caught fire in the storm and burned his whole house down
exercise: What happened to Walt Disney after he died in 1966? | He was cremated | He was frozen to be woken in the future | He was buried under a theme park ride | He was turned into a wax statue
exercise: What did the Wright brothers do first in 1903? | Powered, controlled, sustained flight in an aeroplane | The first time any human ever left the ground | The first hot-air balloon flight | The first flight across an ocean
exercise: How can you check a lone-hero story? | Ask who did it before and who helped | Ask how famous the hero is today | Count how many films are about the hero | Check whether the hero has a statue
test: "einstein failed maths so school doesn't matter innit" What's the truth? | He was brilliant at maths; the story is a myth | He failed maths and later became a doctor | He failed maths but nobody noticed | He never went to school at all, so he couldn't fail any test
test: Mrs Brown says sailors feared falling off the flat Earth. How could you raise it politely? | "Miss, I read people knew it was round; they argued about its size" | "Miss, that's totally wrong, everyone knows that" | "Miss, the flat Earth is true, my cousin said so" | Say nothing, because teachers can never be wrong about anything at all
test: In a cartoon, an apple bonks Newton's head and a light bulb appears. What's closer to the real story? | He saw an apple fall and thought about gravity for years | He was hit and understood gravity in one second | He got the idea from a light bulb he saw | He dropped apples off a tall tower to watch them break on the ground
test: What would have happened if the Americas had not been there? | Columbus's crew would probably have run out of food before reaching Asia | Columbus would have reached China in three days | The ships would have sailed off the edge of the world | The sea would have carried them back to Spain
test: "Edison invented light bulbs, end of" Which is the fairest answer? | Many people made bulbs; Edison's team made a practical one | Edison stole the whole idea and did nothing | Edison invented the light bulb all on his own in a single night | Light bulbs were invented by the Romans
test: Rory's poster says "Columbus discovered America". What is missing? | Millions of people already lived there, and Norse sailors came earlier | Nothing, he was the first person ever to see it | The date, because he really came in 1900 | That he landed in New York first
test: "galileo invented the telescope, duh" What would you say? | It was invented in the Netherlands; he improved it and studied the sky | He invented it all by himself, and also the microscope and the camera | He never used a telescope in his life | He invented it to look at the Moon in 1500
test: Martha says Marie Curie discovered radioactivity alone. Which is fairer? | Becquerel found it; she named it and with Pierre found radium and polonium | She discovered it alone and nobody else worked on it | Pierre Curie did all the work, and she only helped | Einstein discovered it, and she wrote about it
test: Kayden wants to copy Franklin and fly a kite in a thunderstorm. What do you say? | No; lightning can kill, and Franklin was never actually struck | Yes, Franklin was struck and was fine | Yes, but only if the kite is made of paper | Yes, as long as you hold a metal key
test: "disney's frozen in a freezer under disneyland, 100% true" How could you check? | Look up a trusted record: he was cremated in 1966 | Ask on a fan site where everybody already agrees with you | Visit Disneyland and look for the freezer | Believe it, because so many people say it
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the prevailing understanding of the Earth's shape among educated Europeans in the fifteenth century. | The Earth was understood to be spherical | The Earth was understood to be a flat disc | The Earth's shape was regarded as wholly unknowable | The Earth was understood to be cylindrical
exam: Identify the individual to whom the discovery of radioactivity in the year 1896 is properly attributed. | Henri Becquerel | Marie Curie | Albert Einstein | Galileo Galilei
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the contribution of Galileo Galilei in respect of the telescope? | He improved an existing invention and applied it to astronomy | He was the original inventor of the telescope | He prohibited its use for observing the heavens | He employed it solely for the observation of ships approaching harbour
exam: It is given that the Wright brothers achieved a flight in the year 1903. Determine the distinction properly ascribed to that flight. | The first powered, controlled and sustained aeroplane flight | The first ascent of any human being into the air | The first crossing of the Atlantic by air | The first flight of a hot-air balloon
